.\" tty.1 -- maux's tty(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, not copied from a historic
.\" tty(1)), so it carries no third-party notice -- MIT like the
.\" code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build time to
.\" build/man/TTY.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/TTY: `more /usr/man/TTY`.
.TH TTY 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
tty \- print the terminal attached to standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tty
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tty
writes the name of the terminal attached to standard input, one
of
.BR /dev/tty1 " through " /dev/tty4
(the four consoles) or
.BR /dev/ttya0
(the Super Serial Card line). If standard input is not a
terminal,
.I tty
writes
.B "not a tty"
instead. There are no operands; one given is a usage error,
exit status 2.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/TTY  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
stty(1), chvt(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if standard input is a terminal, 1 if it is
not, 2 if standard input's file descriptor could not be
inspected at all (closed, or out of range).
.SH BUGS
.B /dev/null
is a device by this system's own buffering rules but is not a
terminal by any Unix's definition, so
.I tty
reports it as
.BR "not a tty" ,
narrower than the isatty test some other maux code uses for
buffering decisions alone. The historical
.B \-s
option (silence the name, keep only the exit status) is not
implemented -- POSIX.1-2024 itself removed it as obsolescent.
